// Sub-plan F fixture: what is live where a function ends.
//
// An exit block has no successors, so backward liveness needs a seed there,
// and the seed is **everything** — every register, every flag, on every
// function, with no narrowing (`plans/05-optimizer.md` decision 12). Without
// one, the seed is the empty set and the optimizer deletes return values. The
// symptom is a wrong program rather than a compiler error, which is why this
// fixture lands with the block view rather than with the pass that first makes
// the need visible.
//
// **A declared `-> a` is not a licence to call X and Y dead**, tempting as it
// looks. `call foo` is documented as the escape hatch that opts out of
// contract checking, a hand-written `jsr foo` never had a contract to check,
// and an undeclared clobber is `K816-W0123` — a warning, not a guarantee. A
// caller may legally read a register the contract does not list.
//
// Two of the three objections F wrote here are gone. A clause **can** name a
// flag now — `-> a, c` is what `carry` below would need — and an output a
// clause does not list is undefined (sub-plan G, decision 19). What is left
// is the escape hatch: a checked call and `call foo` lower to the same `jsr`,
// so nothing downstream can tell which one a callee was reached through.
//
// G13 measured what the surviving objection costs. Over `examples/`, the
// goldens and the imported corpus, narrowing the seed to what each clause
// declares would delete three instructions and six bytes — one of them the
// `ldy` in `produce` below — while sixty of 72 call sites reach their callee
// through a route that checks no clause at all. `tests/live_outs.rs` holds
// the measurement and `postmortem.md` §G.22 argues it.
//
// `caller` earns `K816-W0123` on purpose: it reads Y after a checked call that
// writes it. That warning is the whole argument — K816 *notices* the
// undeclared clobber and still compiles the read, so the read is legal and Y
// is live.

var CELL = $0400

// `produce` declares `-> a`. The final `lda` survives because it is the
// declared output; the final `ldy` survives **too**, because a caller reached
// through the escape hatch may read Y and the declaration does not forbid it.
func produce @a8 @i8 (a) -> a {
    a+#$01
    ldy #$09
}

// The trailing `clc` survives. A clause could declare the carry now — `-> c`
// is legal since sub-plan G — and this one does not, so the seed keeps it:
// callers set and read the carry as an answer, and no diagnostic told them
// not to.
func carry @a8 @i8 {
    lda #$01
    CELL = a
    clc
}

// The same function called both ways: through the checked form, which states
// the flow, and through the escape hatch, which does not. Both lower to the
// same `jsr`, which is why the optimizer cannot tell them apart afterwards —
// and why it treats every callee's exit the same way.
func caller @a8 @i8 {
    a = #$01
    produce a -> a
    CELL = a
    CELL,y = a

    a = #$02
    call produce
    CELL = a
    CELL,y = a
}

// Mid-function, the identical load goes: `lda #$07` writes A, N, and Z, and
// `lda #$08` overwrites all three before anything reads them. The difference
// from `produce` is only *where* it sits, which is exactly the claim.
func middle @a8 @i8 {
    lda #$07
    lda #$08
    CELL = a
}
